  "worked_solution": "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and the value of $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation, which gives\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[5Y-4].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[5Y-4] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $\\text{E}[X]=11,$ so we solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =5\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-4 \\\\ 15 & =5\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
